Film Format 
Nicole is the narrator, the person organizing the interview clips, video + image files, and case info before presenting the case to the school board. We start off the film when Nicole has already investigated to some degree, enough to report to the school board (which she has done) and she is now going over case files the day before presenting all her findings. 

The order of the scenes in the film isn't the same as the order of the plot line, as in when we start the film, Nicole has already reported (informally) and is now collecting formal data and some of the earlier events is shown through video files she got of like idk, pictures of the teams working together during the montage, old flashbacks of them meeting in the cafe, etc. From the beginning, Nicole is already opening case files, and it is through the case files that we construct the story. At the end of each video clip, she sorts into delete or evidence file

At the end of each file case we view, Nicole pauses and sorts them into delete? Or evidence folder. Here, we can showcase Nicole’s growing inner turmoil as she begins to see the pov of the other students. 

Filmed in clips (formal/informal interview videos, found footage, etc) all done with a video filter (date + time stamp as well and blurry camera texture) in addition to real life portions (no filter) 

When filming/splitting into scenes we want to ensure each scene serves a purpose that makes it feel fast paced or rather just have 2 contradicting emotions/values playing out, main one here I guess would be understanding vs assessing or judging. Each scene should give insight not only to the background of the case but also what that implies for specific characters and relationships.  

